# KNOWN GAP — BOTH BACKENDS. `match` used as an EXPRESSION ignores its arm
# GUARDS entirely and always takes the FIRST arm.
#
#     return match s:
#         case x if x >= 90: "A"
#         case x if x >= 80: "B"
#         case x if x >= 70: "C"
#         else: "F"
#
# grade(85) prints "A" (correct: "B"); grade(10) prints "A" (correct: "F").
# Every input takes arm one, so the guard expressions are evaluated as if they
# were unconditional.
#
# ⚠ THE IDENTICAL GUARDS AS A `match` STATEMENT ARE CORRECT (B / F / A). So the
# discriminator is EXPRESSION-vs-STATEMENT position, not the guards themselves
# and not the scrutinee.
#
# This is a silent wrong-answer defect at rc 0 in a core control-flow construct,
# on textbook code -- a grade calculator is a chapter-2 exercise in every
# language tutorial. Core #8 (a known wrong answer is a defect, never a parity
# excuse) + Core #10.
#
# ⚠ HOW IT WAS FOUND, and why the corpus missed it: an equivalence pair. The
# statement spelling and the expression spelling of the same computation were
# run against each other and disagreed. NO SINGLE SPELLING reveals it -- the
# expression form is self-consistently wrong, and the statement form is right.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): prints B, F, A.
# Found 2026-08-24 by the beginner-code robustness map; orchestrator-verified.

String grade(int s):
    return match s:
        case x if x >= 90: "A"
        case x if x >= 80: "B"
        case x if x >= 70: "C"
        else: "F"

void main():
    print(grade(85))
    print(grade(10))
    print(grade(95))
